  "summary": "Adds the verify-verilex skill, which drives the verilex CLI on a disposable copy of the tally fixture with an isolated VERILEX_HOME.",
  "files": [
    {
      "path": ".cursor/skills/verify-verilex/scripts/launch",
      "summary": "Creates a session: pins the binary under test and copies the tally fixture into a temp dir.",
      "annotations": [
        {
          "newRange": [
            20,
            30
          ],
          "summary": "launch copies the binary into the session, whether --bin gave it or go build made it, and copies tests/fixtures/tally as the scratch product.",
          "rationale": "The binary is copied rather than symlinked, so a later rebuild cannot change a running session, and doctor can compare the sha256 recorded at launch. The fixture is reused rather than duplicated because it is already the maintained example product. The risk is that a test change to the fixture changes the skill's expected outputs. Verified: launch --bin with a separately built frozen binary, cmp against the session copy, and a green run.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-verify-skill"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "path": ".cursor/skills/verify-verilex/scripts/doctor",
      "summary": "Runs the read-only check that a session is ours and worth driving.",
      "annotations": [
        {
          "newRange": [
            13,
            22
          ],
          "summary": "doctor captures --help output before matching it, and fails when files under ~/.local/state/verilex/tally are newer than the session.",
          "rationale": "An earlier `--help | head | grep -q` pipeline under pipefail failed intermittently from SIGPIPE, so the output is now captured first; 8 repeated doctor calls printed ok. The state-dir check catches any write that escaped VERILEX_HOME. If another real tally project writes there during a session, doctor reports a false FAIL.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-verify-skill"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "path": ".cursor/skills/verify-verilex/features/verdicts.md",
      "summary": "Holds the verdict, exit code and honesty-rule recipes.",
      "annotations": [
        {
          "newRange": [
            1,
            54
          ],
          "summary": "The secret probe builds its fake GitHub token at runtime with printf, so no token-shaped literal is ever committed.",
          "rationale": "A literal token pattern in the repo could trip push protection or secret scanners. Every honesty row was driven and printed the listed cause with exit 2. The 7-day expiry in run.md is the one sub-feature that was not driven, because the CLI cannot reach it without waiting. The project secret_patterns probe writes PROBE-1234, a made-up pattern, and restores config.yaml because config changes every stamp.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-verify-skill"
        }
      ]
    },
